Q6 – Deadlock and Inter-Thread Communication
Marks: 7
Bloom’s Level: K4 – Analyse
1. Problem Analysis
This problem contains two parts.

Part A – Hostel Wallet Transfers

Students transfer money between two hostel wallet accounts. Each transfer must lock both accounts to prevent incorrect balance updates. If two threads lock the accounts in opposite orders, deadlock may occur.

Part B – Mess Token Counter

A cook prepares meal tokens, and students collect them.
- The counter can hold a maximum of 5 tokens.
- The cook is the producer.
- Students are consumers.
- The producer waits when the counter is full.
- Consumers wait when the counter is empty.
The main objective is to prevent deadlock and ensure safe communication between threads.
Part A – Deadlock and Prevention
2. Version 1 – Program That Can Deadlock
Consider two wallet accounts, A and B.
- Thread 1 transfers money from A to B.
- Thread 2 transfers money from B to A.
If each thread locks one account and waits for the other, neither can continue.

class Wallet {
    int balance;

    Wallet(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }
}

public class DeadlockDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Wallet A = new Wallet(1000);
        Wallet B = new Wallet(1000);

        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (A) {
                System.out.println("Thread 1 locked A");

                try {
                    Thread.sleep(100);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    return;
                }

                synchronized (B) {
                    A.balance -= 100;
                    B.balance += 100;
                    System.out.println("A to B transfer completed");
                }
            }
        });

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (B) {
                System.out.println("Thread 2 locked B");

                try {
                    Thread.sleep(100);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    return;
                }

                synchronized (A) {
                    B.balance -= 200;
                    A.balance += 200;
                    System.out.println("B to A transfer completed");
                }
            }
        });

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}



Possible output
Thread 1 locked A
Thread 2 locked B


The program may stop here because each thread waits for the lock held by the other.

This example deliberately creates an opportunity for deadlock. The timing is not guaranteed on every run.
3. Four Conditions for Deadlock – Student B
Deadlock can occur when all four conditions are present.
Condition	Meaning	Example in wallet transfer
Mutual exclusion	Only one thread can hold a lock at a time	Only one thread can lock wallet A
Hold and wait	A thread holds one lock while waiting for another	Thread 1 holds A and waits for B
No preemption	A lock cannot be forcibly taken from a thread	Thread 2 keeps its lock on B
Circular wait	Threads wait for one another in a cycle	Thread 1 waits for B; Thread 2 waits for A

All four conditions together make deadlock possible.
4. Version 2 – Deadlock Prevention Using Lock Ordering
A simple solution is to always lock wallet accounts in a consistent order.

For example:
- Lock the account with the lower ID first.
- Lock the account with the higher ID second.
This prevents circular waiting.

class Wallet {
    int id;
    int balance;

    Wallet(int id, int balance) {
        this.id = id;
        this.balance = balance;
    }
}

class SafeTransfer {
    static void transfer(Wallet a, Wallet b, int amount) {
        Wallet first = a.id < b.id ? a : b;
        Wallet second = a.id < b.id ? b : a;

        synchronized (first) {
            synchronized (second) {

                if (a.balance >= amount) {
                    a.balance -= amount;
                    b.balance += amount;

                    System.out.println(
                        "Transferred " + amount +
                        " from Wallet " + a.id +
                        " to Wallet " + b.id);
                } else {
                    System.out.println("Insufficient balance");
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

public class SafeTransferDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args)
            throws InterruptedException {

        Wallet A = new Wallet(1, 1000);
        Wallet B = new Wallet(2, 1000);

        Thread t1 = new Thread(() ->
            SafeTransfer.transfer(A, B, 100));

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() ->
            SafeTransfer.transfer(B, A, 200));

        t1.start();
        t2.start();

        t1.join();
        t2.join();

        System.out.println("Wallet A: " + A.balance);
        System.out.println("Wallet B: " + B.balance);
    }
}



Expected output
Transferred 100 from Wallet 1 to Wallet 2
Transferred 200 from Wallet 2 to Wallet 1
Wallet A: 1100
Wallet B: 900


The order of the two transfer messages may change.

Why does lock ordering work?

Both threads acquire locks in the same order: wallet 1 followed by wallet 2. Therefore, one thread cannot hold wallet 2 while waiting for wallet 1. This removes the circular-wait condition.
Part B – Producer–Consumer Problem
5. Complete TODO 1 and TODO 2
The TokenCounter uses a fixed-size array of 5 positions.
- put() adds a token.
- take() removes a token.
- wait() makes a thread wait when the counter is full or empty.
- notifyAll() wakes waiting threads so they can check the condition again.
Complete Java program

public class TokenCounter {
    private final int[] buffer = new int[5];

    // Counter can hold at most 5 tokens
    private int count = 0, in = 0, out = 0;

    public synchronized void put(int token)
            throws InterruptedException {

        // TODO 1
        while (count == buffer.length) {
            wait();
        }

        buffer[in] = token;
        in = (in + 1) % buffer.length;
        count++;

        System.out.println(
            "Cook added token: " + token +
            " | Counter size: " + count);

        notifyAll();
    }

    public synchronized int take()
            throws InterruptedException {

        // TODO 2
        while (count == 0) {
            wait();
        }

        int token = buffer[out];
        out = (out + 1) % buffer.length;
        count--;

        System.out.println(
            Thread.currentThread().getName() +
            " collected token: " + token +
            " | Counter size: " + count);

        notifyAll();

        return token;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args)
            throws InterruptedException {

        TokenCounter counter = new TokenCounter();

        int[] collected = new int[2];

        Thread cook = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                for (int t = 1; t <= 20; t++) {
                    counter.put(t);
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        });

        Thread[] students = new Thread[2];

        for (int s = 0; s < 2; s++) {
            final int id = s;

            students[s] = new Thread(() -> {
                try {
                    for (int k = 0; k < 10; k++) {
                        collected[id] += counter.take();
                    }
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                }
            }, "Student-" + (s + 1));
        }

        cook.start();

        for (Thread t : students) {
            t.start();
        }

        cook.join();

        for (Thread t : students) {
            t.join();
        }

        System.out.println(
            "Sum of tokens collected: " +
            (collected[0] + collected[1]) +
            " (expected 210)");
    }
}



Expected final output
Sum of tokens collected: 210 (expected 210)


The intermediate token messages can appear in different orders. The final sum should remain 210 if all 20 tokens are successfully collected.
6. Explanation of TODO 1 – put()
while (count == buffer.length) {
    wait();
}

buffer[in] = token;
in = (in + 1) % buffer.length;
count++;

notifyAll();


Explanation:
1. Checks whether the counter is full.
2. If full, the cook waits.
3. Adds the token at the current position.
4. Moves the insertion position using the modulo operator.
5. Increases the counter size.
6. Wakes waiting threads using notifyAll().
7. Explanation of TODO 2 – take()
while (count == 0) {
    wait();
}

int token = buffer[out];
out = (out + 1) % buffer.length;
count--;

notifyAll();

return token;


Explanation:
1. Checks whether the counter is empty.
2. If empty, the student waits.
3. Removes the token from the current position.
4. Moves the removal position.
5. Decreases the counter size.
6. Wakes waiting threads.
7. Returns the collected token.
8. Why Must wait() Be Inside a synchronized Block?
The wait() method must be called by a thread that owns the object's monitor.

Therefore, it must be called inside a synchronized method or synchronized block using the same object.

When a thread calls wait():
- It releases the object's monitor lock.
- It enters the waiting state.
- It waits until notified or interrupted.
- It must reacquire the monitor before continuing.
In this program, both put() and take() are synchronized methods, so they use the same TokenCounter object's monitor.
9. Why Use while Instead of if?
The program uses:
while (count == 0) {
    wait();
}


Instead of:
if (count == 0) {
    wait();
}


Reason:
- A waiting thread may wake up even without a notification (spurious wakeup).
- Another thread may consume the available token before the awakened thread reacquires the lock.
- Therefore, the condition must be checked again before continuing.
The while loop ensures that the thread proceeds only when the condition is actually satisfied.
What happens if we replace while with if?
Consider two students waiting because the counter is empty.
1. The cook adds one token and calls notifyAll().
2. Both students wake up.
3. The first student obtains the lock and removes the token.
4. The second student obtains the lock, but the counter is empty.
5. Because if does not check the condition again, the second student may remove an invalid or already-consumed buffer value.
This can result in incorrect token collection and an incorrect total.

Conclusion: Use while in both put() and take().
10. Difference Between notify() and notifyAll()
notify()	notifyAll()
Wakes one waiting thread	Wakes all waiting threads
The selected thread is not predictable	All waiting threads can compete to reacquire the lock
Can be problematic with different waiting conditions	Safer for this producer–consumer design
May cause progress problems in some designs	Each thread checks its condition again

In this program, notifyAll() is preferred because producers and consumers wait for different conditions.

A thread that wakes up must reacquire the lock and check its while condition. Threads whose conditions are still false go back to waiting.
11. Difference Between sleep() and wait()
sleep()	wait()
Belongs to the Thread class	Belongs to the Object class
Pauses execution for a specified time	Waits for notification, interruption or timeout
Does not release the monitor lock	Releases the monitor lock
Does not require synchronized code	Must own the object's monitor
Used to pause a thread	Used for inter-thread communication

Example:
Thread.sleep(1000);


Pauses the current thread for approximately one second without releasing any monitor it holds.
wait();


Releases the object's monitor and waits for notification.
