13. Reliable Deadlock Experiment – Student B
To make the deadlock demonstration more reliable, use CountDownLatch to ensure both threads have acquired their first locks before either tries to acquire the second.
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;

public class ReliableDeadlock {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Object A = new Object();
        Object B = new Object();

        CountDownLatch ready = new CountDownLatch(2);

        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (A) {
                System.out.println("Thread 1 locked A");
                ready.countDown();

                try {
                    ready.await();
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    return;
                }

                synchronized (B) {
                    System.out.println("Thread 1 locked B");
                }
            }
        });

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (B) {
                System.out.println("Thread 2 locked B");
                ready.countDown();

                try {
                    ready.await();
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    return;
                }

                synchronized (A) {
                    System.out.println("Thread 2 locked A");
                }
            }
        });

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}


Expected output:
Thread 1 locked A
Thread 2 locked B


Both threads then wait forever for the other's lock. This is a deterministic deadlock demonstration under normal execution.

For testing, run this in a separate program because the deadlocked threads will keep the JVM alive.
14. Alternative Concept – ArrayBlockingQueue
Instead of manually implementing wait() and notifyAll(), Java provides ArrayBlockingQueue.

It is a thread-safe bounded queue.

import java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue;

public class QueueDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args)
            throws InterruptedException {

        ArrayBlockingQueue<Integer> counter =
            new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(5);

        int[] collected = new int[2];

        Thread cook = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                for (int t = 1; t <= 20; t++) {
                    counter.put(t);
                    System.out.println("Cook added: " + t);
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        });

        Thread[] students = new Thread[2];

        for (int s = 0; s < 2; s++) {
            final int id = s;

            students[s] = new Thread(() -> {
                try {
                    for (int k = 0; k < 10; k++) {
                        int token = counter.take();
                        collected[id] += token;

                        System.out.println(
                            "Student " + (id + 1) +
                            " collected: " + token);
                    }
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                }
            });
        }

        cook.start();

        for (Thread t : students) {
            t.start();
        }

        cook.join();

        for (Thread t : students) {
            t.join();
        }

        System.out.println(
            "Sum of tokens collected: " +
            (collected[0] + collected[1]) +
            " (expected 210)");

    }
}



Expected final output:
Sum of tokens collected: 210 (expected 210)


Advantages:
- Queue capacity is automatically restricted to 5.
- put() waits when the queue is full.
- take() waits when the queue is empty.
- No manual wait() or notifyAll() is required.
- Less code and fewer synchronization mistakes.
15. Alternative Deadlock Prevention – tryLock()
Instead of using synchronized blocks with a fixed lock order, we can use ReentrantLock.tryLock() with a timeout.
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

class Wallet {
    int balance;
    ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    Wallet(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }
}

class Transfer {
    static boolean transfer(
            Wallet a, Wallet b, int amount)
            throws InterruptedException {

        boolean firstLocked = false;
        boolean secondLocked = false;

        try {
            firstLocked = a.lock.tryLock(1, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

            if (!firstLocked) {
                return false;
            }

            secondLocked = b.lock.tryLock(1, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

            if (!secondLocked) {
                return false;
            }

            if (a.balance < amount) {
                return false;
            }

            a.balance -= amount;
            b.balance += amount;

            return true;
        }
        finally {
            if (secondLocked) {
                b.lock.unlock();
            }

            if (firstLocked) {
                a.lock.unlock();
            }
        }
    }
}


Explanation:
1. The thread tries to acquire the first wallet lock.
2. It tries to acquire the second lock with a timeout.
3. If the second lock is unavailable, it releases the first lock.
4. It can retry the transfer later.
5. Both locks are released safely in the finally block.
This approach avoids waiting forever while holding one lock, provided every transfer follows the timeout and release rule.
16. Time and Space Complexity Analysis
Let:
- n = number of tokens produced and consumed.
- C = buffer capacity, which is 5.
- s = number of wallet accounts.
Producer–consumer operations
Operation	Time complexity	Space complexity
put()	O(1) excluding waiting	O(1) per operation
take()	O(1) excluding waiting	O(1) per operation
ArrayBlockingQueue put()	O(1) excluding waiting	O(C)
ArrayBlockingQueue take()	O(1) excluding waiting	O(C)

For 20 tokens, the total processing work is O(n). Waiting time depends on thread scheduling and production/consumption speed.
Wallet transfer
Approach	Time per transfer	Extra space
Synchronized lock ordering	O(1) work, plus lock waiting	O(1) per transfer
ReentrantLock with timeout	O(1) work, plus lock waiting/retries	O(1) per transfer

The wallet accounts themselves require O(s) space.
17. Which Design Is Less Error-Prone?
Requirement	Recommended design
Producer–consumer counter	ArrayBlockingQueue
Wallet transfers	Consistent lock ordering or timed ReentrantLock
Preventing deadlock	Consistent lock ordering
Avoiding manual wait/notify errors	ArrayBlockingQueue

ArrayBlockingQueue is less error-prone for producer–consumer communication because Java manages the waiting, notification and capacity conditions internally.

For wallet transfers, consistent lock ordering is simple and efficient when all code follows the same ordering rule. Timed tryLock() is useful when an application needs to abandon and retry a transfer instead of waiting indefinitely.
18. Joint Conclusion
Deadlock and producer–consumer problems both involve multiple threads sharing resources and coordinating their work.
- Deadlock occurs when threads wait for locks held by one another.
- Producer–consumer problems occur when threads must wait for available buffer space or data.
- Lock ordering prevents circular waiting.
- wait() and notifyAll() coordinate threads safely.
- ArrayBlockingQueue provides a simpler built-in solution.
Final conclusion: Correct synchronization and communication mechanisms ensure that threads work safely, avoid deadlock and make progress without losing or duplicating tokens.
